1
00:00:03,178 --> 00:00:06,202
Fraud teams get more alerts than they can investigate

2
00:00:06,202 --> 00:00:08,142
and many of them are false alarms.

3
00:00:08,142 --> 00:00:12,649
Graphite investigates each alert through a TigerGraph graph of five hundred and

4
00:00:12,649 --> 00:00:14,589
ninety thousand card transactions.

5
00:00:14,589 --> 00:00:16,187
The model proposes a verdict

6
00:00:16,187 --> 00:00:19,724
and a fixed policy engine decides what the bank actually does.

7
00:00:19,727 --> 00:00:24,044
On the twenty exam cases, the agent called ten fraud, nine legitimate

8
00:00:24,044 --> 00:00:25,483
and left one uncertain.

9
00:00:25,483 --> 00:00:29,737
Each row shows what triggered the alert, the final fraud probability

10
00:00:29,737 --> 00:00:30,738
and the pattern.

11
00:00:30,738 --> 00:00:34,304
Rows marked S A R had a suspicious activity report filed.

12
00:00:34,307 --> 00:00:38,554
We compare three pipelines on one model, G P T O S S one twenty B.

13
00:00:38,554 --> 00:00:42,994
Plain RAG, GraphRAG, and an agent that chooses its own graph queries.

14
00:00:42,994 --> 00:00:47,820
They share the same prompt, the same output, and the same ten policy rules.

15
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:49,300
Only retrieval changes.

16
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:53,547
Every graph query goes through TigerGraph's official M C P server.

17
00:00:53,639 --> 00:00:55,805
First we made the test hard to game.

18
00:00:55,805 --> 00:00:59,837
In the bank's history, the risk score alone gets ninety one percent

19
00:00:59,837 --> 00:01:02,243
so our held out cases are matched on it.

20
00:01:02,243 --> 00:01:04,951
Fraud and cleared cases carry the same score.

21
00:01:04,951 --> 00:01:09,343
On a hundred and sixty eight held out cases, RAG gets forty four percent.

22
00:01:09,343 --> 00:01:11,750
GraphRAG gets seventy three point eight.

23
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:16,383
The agent gets seventy five point six, with the best ranking and calibration.

24
00:01:16,383 --> 00:01:17,045
Same model.

25
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:18,730
The graph is the difference.

26
00:01:18,734 --> 00:01:20,576
Token efficiency is scored too.

27
00:01:20,576 --> 00:01:23,251
RAG uses about thirteen hundred tokens a case

28
00:01:23,251 --> 00:01:27,768
GraphRAG about twenty seven hundred, and the agent about forty eight hundred

29
00:01:27,768 --> 00:01:29,670
over two and a half model calls.

30
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:31,274
The graph does the counting

31
00:01:31,274 --> 00:01:35,197
so the model reads a handful of facts instead of hundreds of rows.

32
00:01:35,199 --> 00:01:36,972
Here is a dispute done right.

33
00:01:36,972 --> 00:01:40,824
The customer says they never made a forty nine dollar purchase.

34
00:01:40,824 --> 00:01:44,921
Before the agent starts, the pipeline checks the card's own history

35
00:01:44,921 --> 00:01:48,344
and finds fifty three earlier forty nine dollar charges.

36
00:01:48,344 --> 00:01:49,812
It's their subscription.

37
00:01:49,812 --> 00:01:51,035
Verdict: legitimate.

38
00:01:51,035 --> 00:01:54,642
Nothing blocked, one model call, under two thousand tokens.

39
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:55,749
Look at the bottom.

40
00:01:55,749 --> 00:01:59,412
Step one is the pencil draft, the actions the model would like.

41
00:01:59,412 --> 00:02:03,947
Step two is the inked verdict: ten written policy rules decide the actions and

42
00:02:03,947 --> 00:02:05,342
who has to approve them.

43
00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:09,586
Here rule R7 closes the case and reminds the customer about the recurring

44
00:02:09,586 --> 00:02:09,993
charge.

45
00:02:09,993 --> 00:02:12,377
The model never blocks a card on its own.

46
00:02:12,378 --> 00:02:13,512
Now a real fraud.

47
00:02:13,512 --> 00:02:19,247
HHG-010 is a one thousand dollar online purchase from a device never seen on this card

48
00:02:19,247 --> 00:02:21,115
seven times its usual spend.

49
00:02:21,115 --> 00:02:23,316
The customer says it wasn't them.

50
00:02:23,316 --> 00:02:26,383
Rule R2 blocks the card, routed to a team lead

51
00:02:26,383 --> 00:02:29,785
and because the exposure is over a thousand dollars

52
00:02:29,785 --> 00:02:32,319
a suspicious activity report is filed.

53
00:02:32,321 --> 00:02:34,005
Here is why the graph matters.

54
00:02:34,005 --> 00:02:36,867
This held out case was a real, legitimate purchase.

55
00:02:36,867 --> 00:02:40,122
Plain RAG read similar sounding notes and called it fraud.

56
00:02:40,122 --> 00:02:42,479
GraphRAG and the agent looked at the graph

57
00:02:42,479 --> 00:02:46,632
saw that alerts in this situation were mostly cleared, and let it through.

58
00:02:59,968 --> 00:03:02,390
Graphite also finds fraud nobody reported.

59
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:03,544
With no alert at all

60
00:03:03,544 --> 00:03:07,639
a sweep links customers who share devices that were new to each of them

61
00:03:07,639 --> 00:03:11,157
and TigerGraph's connected components groups them into rings.

62
00:03:11,157 --> 00:03:15,079
This one links seven customers through twenty three device profiles.

63
00:03:15,079 --> 00:03:19,058
The agent flagged eight cards and filed a suspicious activity report.

64
00:03:19,058 --> 00:03:20,731
Two other rings were cleared.

65
00:03:20,731 --> 00:03:22,634
It doesn't call every ring fraud.

66
00:03:22,637 --> 00:03:25,638
Every finished case is written back into the graph

67
00:03:25,638 --> 00:03:27,738
so the next investigation finds it.

68
00:03:27,738 --> 00:03:32,179
The numbers, every raw answer, and every decision we made are in the repo.

69
00:03:32,179 --> 00:03:33,199
This is Graphite.
